{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>These shall stand to bless the people, etc.</b> And concerning the curses it is not said 'these shall stand to curse,' because blessings come from HaShem in active deed, but curses do not come except of themselves, through the hiding of His face, blessed be He, for 'out of the mouth of the Most High shall not come evil things' (Lamentations 3:38). Therefore it is said 'and these shall stand upon the curse' and not 'to curse,' but rather to forewarn the coming of the curses — and the matter is in doubt whether they will come or not, for since they come only through the hiding of the face, if so the matter still depends on the instruction of the arrangement." "And the fact that all the cursed ones were stated explicitly while the blessings remained in implication and not the reverse — this is because the essence of blessing is for the World to Come, which is hidden, and only from their fruits shall they eat in this world, so that they have the wherewithal to fulfill the commandments by means of the existence of the blessings; therefore Scripture concealed them. And further, it alluded in this that when a man's ways please HaShem, then the curse will be turned for him into a blessing, so that even the cursed ones will be transformed into blessings, in the manner that our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Mo'ed Katan 9a): 'You shall sow and not harvest' — as explained above in parashat Bo (Exodus 10:10) on the verse 'See that evil is before your faces.' And it further alluded that blessing is included within the cursed ones, for since the curse was the cause through whose fear one comes to turn away from evil and do good, if so the blessings were drawn from the direction of the curses." "And the fact that they came in the number eleven — this is because the name יָהּ is the attribute of judgment, as it is said (Psalms 118:18): 'יָּהּ has chastened me sore,' and likewise (Psalms 94:12): 'Happy is the man whom יָּהּ chastenest,' and likewise: 'יָּהּ is my strength and song,' as explained in parashat Beshalah (Exodus 15:2), and it follows that the ו״ה of the great Name is the attribute of mercy. And so Rabbenu Baḥya wrote on the verse 'For a hand is upon the throne of יָהּ' (Exodus 17:16). And the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to create the world with the attribute of judgment — that is, with the name יָהּ — as it is said (Isaiah 26:4): 'For in יָהּ YHWH is an everlasting rock.' And He saw that it could not endure until He also joined ו״ה to יָּהּ. And therefore they said (Bava Batra 9a): one who gives a small coin to a poor person is blessed with six blessings, because his principal reward is in this world, which is received at six extremities — for his reward is from the finest of the heaven above and from the deep lying beneath, and 'you shall spread abroad westward and eastward,' etc. But one who appeases Him with the spiritual speech within him — then his principal reward is for the hidden world, in the name ו״ה, that is, eleven blessings, for it is specifically for the World to Come that the Name is full. Therefore the blessings came in the number eleven. And likewise even the cursed ones — the Holy One, blessed be He, does not send a torrent of wrath immediately, but rather with mercy first, little by little — perhaps they will return — in the manner He did with the generation of the Flood, that if they repented the rains would be rains of blessing. So too even the curses come from the side of mercy, little by little; therefore they too came in the number eleven, which indicates mercy. And for this reason all the reproofs — both those of the Torah of priests and those in this parashah — begin with a ו״ו and end with a ה״א, to teach that even in wrath He remembers mercy." "And in Sotah (37b) they interpreted each of these eleven as applying to an adulterer and adulteress, except for 'the one who perverts the judgment of the stranger, orphan, and widow.' Rashi explained: 'I do not know how to explain it,' but I say that this is its meaning: if a man commits adultery with his fellow's wife and she bears a son from him, and upon [the husband's] death the judge rules that he [the son] inherits together with the other younger brothers — behold he is robbing the orphan, and also diminishing the widow's portion. And the Ibn Ezra wrote that all these matters are done in secret, and so the Ḥizkuni explained, and because the court has no power to stand against them, therefore they banned them with 'cursed.' And this is the correct view, and the proof for it is that the language of secrecy is mentioned only for idolatry, as it is said: 'and sets it up in secret,' and for 'one who strikes his fellow in secret' — for these two encompass the entire Torah and all the commandments, for all are included in these two categories, namely matters between man and the Makom, blessed be He, and matters between man and his fellow. And it specified these eleven to speak of what is prevalent — for with them the matter is common to conceal them, and the same judgment applies to all the commandments of the Torah, which He went back and included in 'cursed be the one who does not establish the words of this Torah.' And what strongly supports this is what they derived: 'who does not establish' — this is one who learns Torah not for its own sake, and that is something done in secret, for he learns for the sake of arrogance, and his intention is not in order to fulfill the commandments — and who can stand upon his mind? And even the ministering angels erred in this, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Bava Metzia 85b): 'For what did the land perish?' — this matter was asked of the sages and the prophets and the angels and they did not explain it, until the Holy One, blessed be He, explained it Himself: 'And He said: Because they have forsaken My Torah,' etc. — for in what was visible they saw them engaged in Torah, but they did not know that they were learning not for its own sake, and when it came to practice they abandoned it, in the manner of the majority of our generation. Therefore He said 'who does not establish the words of this Torah' — what he speaks with his mouth he does not fulfill; infer from this that he learns not for its own sake. And there is no concealment greater than this; therefore they banned him with 'cursed.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {3 "Reading הראב״ע as Ibn Ezra. Abbreviation ה׳ resolved as HaShem throughout. The cryptic phrase 'והוא הדין' in the middle of the Ibn Ezra discussion may be corrupt; rendered literally as 'and the same judgment applies.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:14:29.822983990Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}